UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 29, Lesson 2: Performing a composition', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"To perform a composition, rehearse until each part is secure. Check the score, decide who leads and count in so everyone begins together. Keep the beat steady, follow the dynamics and tempo written in the score and finish together on a clear signal. After the performance, listen to the response from the audience. If you can record it with an adult''s permission, listen back to hear the dynamics and tempo. Reflect on what matched your plan and what you would change.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite the steps for a leader to start, conduct and finish a group composition.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Count in, give a clear hand cue for each change, keep the beat steady and close the hand to finish.'' A clear count-in, cues and finish keep the group together.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRehearse and perform your composition from last lesson for family. Use a count-in and a finish signal. Ask them for two stars and a wish and, with permission, record it to listen to. Write one change you would make.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"To perform a composition, rehearse until each part is secure. Check the score, decide who leads and count in so everyone begins together. Keep the beat steady, follow the dynamics and tempo written in the score and finish together on a clear signal. After the performance, listen to the response from the audience. If you can record it with an adult''s permission, listen back to hear the dynamics and tempo. Reflect on what matched your plan and what you would change.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Create and perform","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-MUS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Rehearse and perform your composition from last lesson for family. Use a count-in and a finish signal. Ask them for two stars and a wish and, with permission, record it to listen to. Write one change you would make. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Count in, give a clear hand cue for each change, keep the beat steady and close the hand to finish.'' A clear count-in, cues and finish keep the group together. Reasoning: I used a performance by attending to a count-in rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Believing that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve. Correction: Strong answer: ''Count in, give a clear hand cue for each change, keep the beat steady and close the hand to finish.'' A clear count-in, cues and finish keep… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a performance.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a performance on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a performance, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Count in, give a clear hand cue for each change, keep the beat steady and close the hand to finish.'' A clear count-in, cues and finish keep… This matches the lesson skill for a performance.","options":["Believing that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Count in, give a clear hand cue for each change, keep the beat steady and close the hand to finish.'' A clear count-in, cues and finish keep…"],"question":"Write the steps for a leader to start, conduct and finish a group composition. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Believing that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of a count-in with clear evidence.","Believing that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve.","To perform a composition, rehearse until each part is secure. Check the score, decide who leads and count in so everyone begins together. Keep the beat…","Strong answer: ''Count in, give a clear hand cue for each change, keep the beat steady and close the hand to finish.'' A clear count-in, cues and finish keep…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a performance?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named a count-in, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the a count-in used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance."],"question":"A learner is practising a performance. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Count in, give a clear hand cue for each change, keep the beat steady and close the hand to finish.'' A clear count-in, cues and finish keep… uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Count in, give a clear hand cue for each change, keep the beat steady and close the hand to finish.'' A clear count-in, cues and finish keep…","Believing that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a performance?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Believing that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a performance.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about a count-in?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Believing that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Example: Ravi leads his group''s piece ''Playground''. He counts ''1, 2, 3, 4'', and they play section A softly. For B he raises his hand for a…"],"question":"Using this lesson idea — To perform a composition, rehearse until each part is secure. Check the score, decide who leads and count in… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Rehearse and perform your composition from last lesson for family. Use a count-in and a finish signal. Ask them for two stars and a wish and, with…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Believing that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a performance?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a performance on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Count in, give a clear hand cue for each change, keep the beat steady and close the hand to finish.'' A clear count-in, cues and finish keep the group together. Reasoning: I used a performance by attending to a count-in rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a performance, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Believing that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve. Correction: Strong answer: ''Count in, give a clear hand cue for each change, keep the beat steady and close the hand to finish.'' A clear count-in, cues and finish keep… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a performance.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w29-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a performance.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Music","success_criteria":["I can use a performance with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a count-in and explain why it matters.","I can practise a conductor independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a recording needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"To perform a composition, rehearse until each part is secure. Check the score, decide who leads and count in so everyone begins together. Keep the beat steady, follow the dynamics and tempo written in the score and finish together on a clear signal. After the performance,…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","To perform a composition, rehearse until each part is secure. Check the score, decide who leads and count in so everyone begins together. Keep the beat steady, follow the dynamics and tempo written in the score and finish together on a clear signal. After the performance, listen to the response from the audience. If you can record it with an adult''s permission, listen back to hear the dynamics and tempo. Reflect on what matched your plan and what you would change.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a performance?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ravi leads his group''s piece ''Playground''. He counts ''1, 2, 3, 4'', and they play section A softly. For B he raises his hand for a crescendo, and the group plays louder. He lowers his hand for the return of A. They finish on his closed fist. Afterwards they listen to a recording and notice the crescendo was too quick, so they plan to slow it.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the steps for a leader to start, conduct and finish a group composition.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Count in, give a clear hand cue for each change, keep the beat steady and close the hand to finish.'' A clear count-in, cues and finish keep the group together.","explain":"To perform a composition, rehearse until each part is secure. Check the score, decide who leads and count in so everyone begins together. Keep the beat steady, follow the dynamics and tempo written in the score and finish together on a clear signal. After the performance, listen to the response from the audience. If you can record it with an adult''s permission, listen back to hear the dynamics and tempo. Reflect on what matched your plan and what you would change.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A count-in and cue.","caption":"Lead clearly.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Count in</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">1, 2, 3, 4</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cue</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">raise hand for louder</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Ravi leads his group''s piece ''Playground''. He counts ''1, 2, 3, 4'', and they play section A softly. For B he rai Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Ravi leads his group''s piece ''Playground''. He counts ''1, 2, 3, 4'', and they play section A softly. For B he rai Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a performance?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ravi leads his group''s piece ''Playground''. He counts ''1, 2, 3, 4'', and they play section A softly. For B he raises his hand for a crescendo, and the group plays louder. He lowers his hand for the return of A. They finish on his closed fist. Afterwards they listen to a recording and notice the crescendo was too quick, so they plan to slow it. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Performing and reflecting.","caption":"Reflect after playing.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Perform</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">steady beat, follow the plan</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Reflect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">listen back, change one thing</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a performance?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Ravi leads his group''s piece ''Playground''. He counts ''1, 2, 3, 4'', and they play section A softly. For B he raises his hand for a crescendo, and the group plays louder. He lowers his hand for the return of A. They finish on his closed fist. Afterwards they listen to a recording and notice the crescendo was too quick, so they plan to slow it.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Count in, give a clear hand cue for each change, keep the beat steady and close the hand to finish.'' A clear count-in, cues and finish keep the group together.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Believing that the performance is the end of the work. Reflecting on what worked and what to change is part of composing, because the next version can improve. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a performance?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTo perform a composition, rehearse until each part is secure. Check the score, decide who leads and count in so everyone begins together. Keep the beat steady, follow the dynamics and tempo written in the score and finish together on a clear signal. After the performance, listen to the response from the audience. If you can record it with an adult''s permission, listen back to hear the dynamics and tempo. Reflect on what matched your plan and what you would change. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 29, Lesson 2: Performing a composition","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Singing, instruments and composing","week":29,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Ravi leads his group''s piece ''Playground''. He counts ''1, 2, 3, 4'', and they play section A softly. For B he raises his hand for a crescendo, and the group plays louder. He lowers his hand for the return of A. They finish on his closed fist. Afterwards they listen to a recording and notice the crescendo was too quick, so they plan to slow it.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A count-in and cue.","caption":"Lead clearly.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Count in</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">1, 2, 3, 4</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cue</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">raise hand for louder</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Performing and reflecting.","caption":"Reflect after playing.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Perform</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">steady beat, follow the plan</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Reflect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">listen back, change one thing</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w29-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
